2. Types of Variables in Java (Big Picture)
Based on Scope:
Local variables
Instance variables
Static (class) variables

Based on Modifiers / Behavior:
final
static
volatile
transient

3. Local Variables
What is a Local Variable?
Declared inside a method, constructor, or block
Scope limited to that block
No default value (must initialize)

Example
class Test {
    void show() {
        int x = 10; // local variable
        System.out.println(x);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new Test().show();
    }
}

Output
10

Key Points:
Stored in stack
Lifetime = method execution
❌ Cannot be static

4. Instance Variables (Non-Static)
What is an Instance Variable?
Declared inside class but outside methods
Each object has its own copy
Gets default values

Example
class Person {
    String name; // instance variable

    void show() {
        System.out.println(name);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Person p = new Person();
        p.show();
    }
}

Output
null

Default Values:
Type	  Default
----    -------
int      	  0
boolean	  false
Object	  null

5. Static Variables (Class Variables)
What is a Static Variable?
Declared using static
One copy per class
Shared by all objects
Stored in method area

Example
class Counter {
    static int count = 0;

    Counter() {
        count++;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new Counter();
        new Counter();
        System.out.println(count);
    }
}

Output
2

Key Points:
Initialized when class loads
Accessed using class name
Used for common/shared data

6. Final Variables
What is a Final Variable?
Value cannot be changed
Must be initialized once

6.1 Final Local Variable
final int x = 10;
// x = 20; ❌

6.2 Final Instance Variable
class Test {
    final int x = 10;
}

6.3 Blank Final Variable
class Test {
    final int x;

    Test() {
        x = 10;
    }
}

6.4 Final Reference Variable (🔥 Tricky)
import java.util.*;

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        final List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
        list.add("Java");      // ✔ allowed
        // list = new ArrayList<>(); ❌
        System.out.println(list);
    }
}

Output
[Java]

👉 Reference is final, object is mutable

7. Static Final Variables (Constants 🔥)
Example
class Constants {
    public static final int MAX = 100;
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(Constants.MAX);
    }
}

Output
100

Why static final?
One copy
Immutable
Compile-time constant

8. Volatile Variable (Multithreading 🔥)
What is volatile?
Ensures visibility across threads
Value read from main memory
❌ Does NOT ensure atomicity

Example
class Test {
    volatile boolean flag = true;
}

Use Case:
Status flags
Shutdown signals

9. Transient Variable (Serialization 🔥)
What is transient?
Variable not serialized
Used with Serializable

Example
import java.io.*;

class User implements Serializable {
    String name;
    transient String password;

    User(String name, String password) {
        this.name = name;
        this.password = password;
    }
}
👉 password will not be saved to file

10. Variable Scope Summary (🔥 Important Table)
| Variable Type | Scope        | Lifetime         | Memory      |
| ------------- | ------------ | ---------------- | ----------- |
| Local         | Method/block | Method execution | Stack       |
| Instance      | Object       | Object lifetime  | Heap        |
| Static        | Class        | JVM lifetime     | Method Area |

| Feature  | Static      | Instance     |
| -------- | ----------- | ------------ |
| Copy     | One         | Per object   |
| Access   | Class name  | Object       |
| Memory   | Method area | Heap         |
| Use case | Shared data | Object state |

| Feature  | Static      | Instance     |
| -------- | ----------- | ------------ |
| Copy     | One         | Per object   |
| Access   | Class name  | Object       |
| Memory   | Method area | Heap         |
| Use case | Shared data | Object state |

17. JVM & Memory Perspective:
Local variables → Stack frame
Instance variables → Heap
Static variables → Method area
Final primitives → Inlined by compiler
Volatile → Main memory visibility

18. Common Tricky Interview Questions (4+ Years)
Q1. Do local variables get default values?
❌ No

Q2. Can static variable be final?
✅ Yes (constants)

Q3. Does final guarantee immutability?
❌ No (object may be mutable)

Q4. Can volatile ensure thread safety?
❌ No (only visibility)

Q5. Why constants are static final?
Single copy + immutable + fast access
